---
// Prototype: landing for people who come from the WordPress.org listing of Loopress Light, already
// installed, asking "what does this do with the CLI, and what's Full?". Facts from
// wordpress-plugin/flavors/light/readme.txt and cli/src/commands/project/config.ts (the Full
// install is a prompt, declining keeps Light). Throwaway, not linked.
import DocPage from "@/components/proto/DocPage.astro";

export const prerender = true;

const synced = [
  ["ACF field groups, post types, taxonomies, options pages", "lps acf pull · push"],
  ["SEO settings and redirects (Yoast, Rank Math)", "lps seo pull · push"],
  ["Navigation menus and their items", "lps menu pull · push"],
  ["The options you choose to track", "lps option pull · push"],
  ["Block theme styles", "lps theme-styles pull · push"],
];
---

<DocPage title="Loopress Light - Loopress" kicker="loopress light · from wordpress.org" heading="You installed Light. Here's the other half." lede="Light is the plugin. The workflow lives in the CLI: it turns your site's configuration into files you keep in Git, review, and push to any environment.">
  <h2>What Light puts in Git</h2>
  <table class="synced">
    <tbody>
      {synced.map(([what, cmd]) => (
        <tr><td>{what}</td><td class="cmd">{cmd}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="aside">no code, by design: light only moves configuration, through the same APIs you use by hand.</p>

  <h2>Three commands to start</h2>
  <pre>$ npm i -g @loopress/cli
$ lps project config     # authorize in your browser
$ lps pull               # your site's configuration, as files</pre>
  <p>
    <code>lps project config</code> will offer to install Loopress Full. Say no and you stay on Light,
    everything above keeps working.
  </p>

  <h2>Then, the loop</h2>
  <p>
    Commit the files. Change a field group locally, open a PR, run <code>lps diff --env staging</code>
    to see what would change, then <code>lps push</code>. When someone edits a menu in wp-admin,
    <code>lps pull</code> brings it back into the repo. Posts, orders and users never move.
  </p>

  <h2>When you want Full</h2>
  <p>
    Full is the same plugin with the code half switched on: hooks, API routes, apps, pages, snippets,
    and a plugin lockfile. It is free too. WordPress.org doesn't host plugins that deploy code, so Full
    ships from loopress.dev, and installing it replaces Light. Re-run <code>lps project config</code>
    and accept the prompt.
  </p>

  <div class="ctas">
    <a href="https://docs.loopress.dev/getting-started/" class="btn">Get started</a>
    <a href="/proto/loop-12">See the whole loop</a>
  </div>
</DocPage>

<style>
  .synced { width: 100%; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .synced td { padding: 0.75rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in oklch, currentColor 15%, transparent); vertical-align: top; }
  .synced .cmd { text-align: right; padding-left: 1rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .ctas { margin-top: 3rem; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 1.25rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; height: 2.9rem; padding-inline: 1.4rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; background: oklch(0.22 0.05 255); color: oklch(0.975 0.008 95); font-weight: 800; text-decoration: none !important; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .synced .cmd { white-space: normal; } }
</style>
